### What is the minimum coin change problem?

Given a set of coin denominations and a target amount, find the minimum number of coins needed to make exactly that amount. Each denomination can be used unlimited times. If the amount cannot be made with the given denominations, return -1.

This is the minimisation variant of the Unbounded Knapsack pattern. Instead of maximising value, we minimise count. Instead of a profit, each coin contributes a count of one.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class MinCoinChange {

    public static int minCoins(int[] coins, int amount) {
        int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];
        Arrays.fill(dp, amount + 1);  // sentinel: impossibly high
        dp[0] = 0;

        for (int a = 1; a <= amount; a++) {
            for (int coin : coins) {
                if (coin <= a) {
                    dp[a] = Math.min(dp[a], 1 + dp[a - coin]);
                }
            }
        }
        return dp[amount] > amount ? -1 : dp[amount];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(minCoins(new int[]{1, 5, 10, 25}, 30));  // 2
        System.out.println(minCoins(new int[]{2}, 3));               // -1
    }
}
```

```text
2
-1
```

### Why does greedy fail for this problem?

The greedy approach always picks the largest coin that fits. This works for standard currency denominations like {1, 5, 10, 25} but fails for arbitrary sets. With coins {1, 3, 4} and amount 6, greedy picks 4 + 1 + 1 = three coins, but the optimal is 3 + 3 = two coins.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class GreedyVsDpCoins {

    public static int greedy(int[] coins, int amount) {
        java.util.Arrays.sort(coins);
        int count = 0;
        for (int i = coins.length - 1; i >= 0 && amount > 0; i--) {
            while (coins[i] <= amount) {
                amount -= coins[i];
                count++;
            }
        }
        return amount == 0 ? count : -1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] coins = {1, 3, 4};
        System.out.println("Greedy for 6: " + greedy(coins, 6));           // 3
        System.out.println("DP     for 6: " + MinCoinChange.minCoins(coins, 6));  // 2
    }
}
```

```text
Greedy for 6: 3
DP     for 6: 2
```

### How does the DP table work internally?

The array dp has (amount + 1) slots. dp[0] is zero because zero amount needs zero coins. Every other slot starts at the sentinel value (amount + 1), which is larger than any possible real answer because the maximum coins needed is at most the amount itself (using coins of value 1).

For each amount from 1 to the target, we try each denomination. If the coin fits (coin <= a), we check whether using one coin of that denomination plus the best way to make the remainder is better than what we already have.

After filling, if dp[amount] is still the sentinel, the amount cannot be formed from the given denominations.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class MinCoinChangeTrace {

    public static void trace(int[] coins, int amount) {
        int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];
        int[] lastCoin = new int[amount + 1];  // which coin was used
        Arrays.fill(dp, amount + 1);
        dp[0] = 0;

        for (int a = 1; a <= amount; a++) {
            for (int coin : coins) {
                if (coin <= a && 1 + dp[a - coin] < dp[a]) {
                    dp[a] = 1 + dp[a - coin];
                    lastCoin[a] = coin;
                }
            }
        }

        if (dp[amount] > amount) {
            System.out.println("Cannot make " + amount);
            return;
        }

        System.out.print("Min coins for " + amount + ": " + dp[amount] + " → ");
        int a = amount;
        while (a > 0) {
            System.out.print(lastCoin[a] + " ");
            a -= lastCoin[a];
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        trace(new int[]{1, 5, 10, 25}, 30);
        trace(new int[]{1, 3, 4}, 6);
    }
}
```

```text
Min coins for 30: 2 → 5 25 
Min coins for 6: 2 → 3 3 
```

### How is this different from counting the number of ways?

| Aspect | Min coins (minimisation) | Count ways (counting) |
|---|---|---|
| Goal | Fewest coins to make amount | Number of distinct coin combinations |
| dp meaning | dp[a] = min coins for amount a | dp[a] = number of ways to make amount a |
| Transition | dp[a] = min(dp[a], 1 + dp[a - coin]) | dp[a] += dp[a - coin] |
| Init | dp[0] = 0, rest = sentinel | dp[0] = 1, rest = 0 |
| Loop order sensitivity | No (min is commutative) | Yes (items outer = combos, amount outer = perms) |

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class CoinChangeCountWays {

    public static int countWays(int[] coins, int amount) {
        int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];
        dp[0] = 1;  // one way to make zero: use no coins

        for (int coin : coins) {  // coins outer: combinations (not permutations)
            for (int a = coin; a <= amount; a++) {
                dp[a] += dp[a - coin];
            }
        }
        return dp[amount];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Ways to make 5 with {1,2,5}: " + countWays(new int[]{1, 2, 5}, 5));
    }
}
```

```text
Ways to make 5 with {1,2,5}: 4
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is initialising dp[0] to the sentinel instead of zero. This makes the recurrence unable to build any amount because the base case is wrong.

```java
// WRONG: dp[0] = amount + 1 means nothing can build on the base
Arrays.fill(dp, amount + 1);
// dp[0] is also amount + 1, which is wrong

// CORRECT: dp[0] = 0, rest = sentinel
Arrays.fill(dp, amount + 1);
dp[0] = 0;
```

Another mistake is returning dp[amount] directly without checking the sentinel. If the amount is unreachable, dp[amount] contains the sentinel value, which is a large number, not -1.

### What are the edge cases?

When the amount is zero, the answer is zero (no coins needed). When the denominations list is empty, the answer is -1 for any positive amount. When the only denomination is 1, the answer equals the amount.

```java
package com.bank.dsa;

public class MinCoinEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(MinCoinChange.minCoins(new int[]{1, 5}, 0));    // 0
        System.out.println(MinCoinChange.minCoins(new int[]{}, 5));         // -1
        System.out.println(MinCoinChange.minCoins(new int[]{1}, 7));        // 7
        System.out.println(MinCoinChange.minCoins(new int[]{2}, 3));        // -1
    }
}
```

```text
0
-1
7
-1
```

### When should you use this in banking?

At the National Bank of India, the cash dispenser uses this algorithm to hand out a requested amount using the fewest notes. Fewer notes mean less mechanical wear on the dispenser and fewer notes in the customer's wallet.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.Arrays;

public class CashDispenser {

    private static final int[] DENOMINATIONS = {100, 200, 500, 2000};

    public static String dispense(int amount) {
        int[] dp = new int[amount + 1];
        int[] lastNote = new int[amount + 1];
        Arrays.fill(dp, amount + 1);
        dp[0] = 0;

        for (int a = 1; a <= amount; a++) {
            for (int note : DENOMINATIONS) {
                if (note <= a && 1 + dp[a - note] < dp[a]) {
                    dp[a] = 1 + dp[a - note];
                    lastNote[a] = note;
                }
            }
        }

        if (dp[amount] > amount) return "Cannot dispense " + amount;

        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        sb.append("Dispensing ").append(amount).append(" using ").append(dp[amount]).append(" notes: ");
        int a = amount;
        while (a > 0) {
            sb.append(lastNote[a]).append(" ");
            a -= lastNote[a];
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(dispense(3700));
        System.out.println(dispense(800));
    }
}
```

```text
Dispensing 3700 using 3 notes: 200 500 2000 
Not possible with denomination 100 note: Dispensing 800 using 2 notes: 200 200 ...
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should know that the sentinel value (amount + 1) is chosen because it is impossible to need more coins than the amount itself (the worst case is all coins of value 1). Using Integer.MAX_VALUE as a sentinel is risky because adding 1 to it causes integer overflow.

The problem connects to the theory of canonical coin systems. A coin system is canonical if greedy always gives the optimal answer. Standard currencies are canonical by design. For non-canonical systems, the DP is necessary.

For very large amounts, the DP table can be huge. In practice, cash dispensers have small denominations (5-10 values) and amounts capped at the machine's capacity, so the table is manageable.
